---
title: "Stepping through an algorithm"
description: "A `::: {.debug}` block turns a real run into a steppable, visual trace: bars, boxes, a grid and pointer carets, built from the execution itself, not drawn by hand."
---

A `::: {.debug}` block runs one code cell under a real tracer and lets a reader step
through the recording: forward, back, to a specific step, or on autoplay. Every picture
on this page came from an actual run of the code beside it, not from a screenshot: reload
the page and step it yourself.

## The minimal block

The smallest version is one fenced div around one traced cell. No JavaScript, no
configuration beyond `#| trace: true`:

::: {.debug}
```{python}
#| trace: true
def find_max(a):
    best = a[0]
    for i in range(1, len(a)):
        if a[i] > best:
            best = a[i]
    return best

find_max([30, 70, 20, 90, 40])
```
:::

Step forward a few times with the transport bar (or the arrow keys once you have clicked
into the widget) and watch three things happen together: the line cursor in the code
panel moves to whatever line is about to run, `a` renders as bars because it is a plain
list of numbers, and `i` grows a labelled pointer caret under its slot because it is an
integer that happens to be a valid index into `a`. `best` shows up only in the variables
panel: it is a number too, but at no step is its value also a valid index, so it earns no
caret of its own, which is the whole rule, being in range is the entire signal, not a
variable name Taliesin was told to watch for.

That is the complete feature with zero authored JavaScript: a `#| trace: true` cell inside
`::: {.debug}` is enough for the transport bar, the line cursor, the variables panel, and
whichever of the four automatic pictures (bars, boxes, a grid, or a pointer caret) fits
each traced value's shape.

## Naming a block: `name=`

Add `name="…"` when something *else* on the page needs to know what step the reader is
on. Naming does two things at once: the block starts publishing its current step index as
a reactive input under that name (the same mechanism `.scrolly` uses), and it registers
its trace where `tali.frame(name)` can find it from any other `{js}` cell. Without a
name, the stepper still runs; it is just self-contained, nothing outside the block can
read it.

## Taking over the picture

A separate `{js}` cell can read the current step with `tali.frame(name)` and draw its own
picture. This does not remove the automatic one: a `{js}` cell like this one mounts its
own output wherever it sits on the page, so treat it as a second, purpose-built view
alongside the general-purpose one, not a replacement for it.

::: {.debug name="pairsum"}
```{python}
#| trace: true
def pair_sum(a, target):
    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1
    while lo < hi:
        s = a[lo] + a[hi]
        if s == target:
            return (lo, hi)
        if s < target:
            lo += 1
        else:
            hi -= 1
    return None

pair_sum([1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11], 12)
```
:::

```{js}
//| input: pairsum
const f = tali.frame("pairsum");
const a = f.locals.a || [];
const row = document.createElement("div");
row.style.cssText = "display:flex;gap:.35rem;font-family:monospace;";
a.forEach((v, i) => {
  const cell = document.createElement("span");
  cell.textContent = v;
  const pointed = i === f.locals.lo || i === f.locals.hi;
  cell.style.cssText =
    "padding:.3rem .5rem;border-radius:.35rem;" +
    (pointed
      ? "background:#f97316;color:#fff;"
      : "background:#334155;color:#e2e8f0;");
  row.appendChild(cell);
});
return row;
```

That view cell reads two things off the frame: `f.locals.a`, the array, and
`f.locals.lo`/`f.locals.hi`, the two pointers, then paints only the pointed-at cells.

::: {.callout-warning}
## `tali.frame` never returns null, guard the KEYS instead

`tali.frame(name)` never returns `null`, even on the very first render, before this
block's trace exists yet, it hands back an empty, frame-shaped stand-in instead:
`locals` is `{}`, `changed` is `{}`, `line` is `null`. So `f.changed`, `f.locals`, and
`f.line` are always safe to read; there is nothing to null-check on `f` itself.

What is *not* always there is a given key: `f.locals.a` is `undefined` until the traced
run actually binds a local named `a`, and it stays `undefined` forever if you typo the
name. That is why the view cell above writes `f.locals.a || []` rather than `f.locals.a`,
the exact same fallback you would reach for mid-trace, before a loop has assigned a
variable yet. One idiom covers both cases: "not mounted yet" and "not bound yet" look
identical from a view cell's perspective, so they take the same guard.
:::

## The JS generator form

A traced cell can be `{js}` instead of `{python}`. Each `yield` is one recorded step,
captured entirely in the reader's browser: no kernel, no build-time execution. Because
the capture runs client-side, the reader can change an input and the trace re-captures at
the new value, something the `{python}` form cannot do (a kernel trace is fixed once the
page is built).

{{< input name="n" type="slider" min="4" max="9" step="1" value="6" label="array size" >}}

::: {.debug name="live"}
```{js}
//| trace: true
//| input: n

function shuffled(count) {
  const a = Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => i + 1);
  for (let i = a.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [a[i], a[j]] = [a[j], a[i]];
  }
  return a;
}

function* bubbleSort(a) {
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    for (let j = 0; j < a.length - i - 1; j++) {
      yield { a, i, j };
      if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) [a[j], a[j + 1]] = [a[j + 1], a[j]];
    }
}

return bubbleSort(shuffled(tali.value("n")));
```
:::

Move the slider: the traced cell's own `//| input: n` is what tells it to re-run and
re-capture at the new size, the same `//| input:` any other reactive `{js}` cell uses.
The transport resets to step 1 of a new total, because it genuinely is a new trace, a
different shuffle at a different length, not the old one resumed.

## Caps and what truncation looks like

A trace is capped at 5,000 steps, the same limit for both the `{python}` and `{js}`
adapters. An algorithm meant to be *watched* one step at a time should stay small, every
example on this page traces 5 to 8 elements, on purpose: the picture is for reading, not
for scrolling past. If a traced cell does produce more than 5,000 steps, the recording
stops there rather than growing without bound, and a line reading `Trace truncated at
5000 steps.` appears above the widget. Stepping still works normally up to that point; it
is a stop, not a failure.

## Full screen

The transport bar's last control, **Expand**, fullscreens the debug widget itself, not
the whole page, using the browser's Fullscreen API. It is the button to reach for on a
small screen or with a wide picture, a many-column DP grid, a long array of bars, that
would otherwise be cramped beside the code panel. The button's label flips to **Collapse**
while active, and the Escape key (or the browser's own fullscreen exit) leaves it the same
way any fullscreen element exits.
